  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nWith a northerly flow continuing on the eastern periphery of \nhigh amplitude ridging over the central CONUS and extending well\nnorthward into central Canada, a band of lake effect \nprecipitation continues to stream southward into northwestern IN\nand northeastern IL. Currently surface temperatures have warmed\nenough to keep all precipitation in liquid form. Tonight, a \nslight shift in winds to more northwesterly around 4 to 5 AM EST\nwill allow the band to make a more easterly shift further into \nnorthwestern IN (northwestern portions of the CWA) and along \nwith the diurnal cooling will start to see snow showers mix in \nespecially further from Lake Michigan. Minor accumulations \ngenerally around a tenth to a trace of snowfall will be expected\non grassy areas. After 10 AM EST, diurnal warming will once \nagain switch precipitation over to mostly rain showers through \nthe remainder of Saturday. Some of the showers will make it \nfurther inland by Saturday afternoon but will see a gradual \ndecrease in coverage as winds weaken with eastward progression \nof the ridging into the western portions of the area by Saturday\nnight into Sunday morning.\n\nThe high amplitude ridging will continue to slowly push \neastward into next week allowing for a return to dry weather\nalong with a gradual warming trend. High temperatures will\nremain in the mid to upper 40s on Saturday and Sunday with\nslight warming each day when Monday will get back into the 50s\nthrough midweek. By Thursday and Friday, highs will again push\nback into the low to mid 60s.",
